DEFECT 2: Verdict text is nonsensical for false-start results.

In render() at the "result" phase, the verdict is calculated unconditionally:
  let verdict = ms < 200 ? "Too fast — were you guessing?" : ...

For a false start, last.ms is null. In JavaScript, null < 200 evaluates to true (null coerced to 0), so the verdict always shows "Too fast — were you guessing?" for false starts. This is displayed in the subtitle below the "False start!" title.

Fix: Guard the verdict calculation to only run for valid trials:
  let verdict = "";
  if (!last.falseStart) {
    verdict = ms < 200 ? "Too fast — were you guessing?" :
              ms < 250 ? "Quick reflexes!" :
              ms < 350 ? "Solid reaction." : "Take your time next time.";
  }

DEFECT 3: Average shows 0ms when all trials are false starts.

In renderDone():
  const valid = results.filter(r => !r.falseStart).map(r => r.ms);
  const avg = valid.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / (valid.length || 1);

When all 5 trials are false starts, valid is []. reduce returns 0 (initial value). valid.length || 1 is 1. So avg = 0 / 1 = 0. The summary displays "Average: 0ms" which is incorrect.

Fix: Show dash when there are no valid trials:
  const avg = valid.length ? Math.round(valid.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / valid.length) : null;
And update the template to show dash for null avg.

ADDITIONAL FINDING: setDir checks reversal against `dir` instead of `nextDir`.

Evidence in code:
  function setDir(x, y) {
      if (x === -dir.x && y === -dir.y) return;
      nextDir = { x, y };
  }

Problem: dir is only updated inside step() at the beginning of each game tick. Between key presses that happen before the next tick, the check is against the stale dir value. This allows a player to reverse direction by pressing two opposite keys in quick succession.

Example scenario:
- Snake moving right (dir = {x:1, y:0})
- Player presses Up: setDir(0,-1) -> check 0===-1? No. nextDir = {0,-1}
- Before next tick, player presses Down: setDir(0,1) -> check 0===-1? No! nextDir = {0,1}
- Next step: dir = nextDir = {0,1}. Snake reverses into itself!

Fix: Change the check to use nextDir:
  function setDir(x, y) {
      if (x === -nextDir.x && y === -nextDir.y) return;
      nextDir = { x, y };
  }

This ensures the reversal check is against the last requested direction, preventing the rapid-reverse exploit.

Also verified: all three previously identified bugs are confirmed fixed:
1. startGame: lastTime = performance.now() (was lastTime = 0)
2. togglePause(resume): lastTime = performance.now() (was lastTime = 0)
3. R key handler: has e.preventDefault() (was missing)

All brief requirements verified: keyboard controls (Arrows/WASD), food, scoring, wall/self collisions, pause (Space), restart (R/button), increasing difficulty, single self-contained offline index.html.

PIXEL PAINTER - SECOND FEEDBACK (post-repair review)

The redo fix and resize content-preservation fix look correct. However, I found an additional defect:

=== DEFECT 3: Touch handling on canvas has no touchcancel listener ===
The app registers touchstart/touchmove/touchend but does NOT register a "touchcancel" handler. If the browser cancels the touch (e.g., due to a modal alert, system intervention, or scrolling trigger), the `painting` flag stays true and pointer events on subsequent touches would be mishandled. This is a low-probability but real defect for mobile/touch users.

=== DEFECT 4: initCanvas() resize content preservation has incorrect intermediate canvas sizing ===
In initCanvas(c, r, keep) when keep=true:
  const prev = document.createElement("canvas");
  prev.width = cols;   // BUG: cols is already set to NEW size c!
  prev.height = rows;  // same issue — should be OLD cols/rows
  prev.getContext("2d").putImageData(imageData, 0, 0);

The imageData buffer has the OLD canvas dimensions (the old cols×rows that existed before initCanvas was called). But `cols` and `rows` have ALREADY been overwritten to the NEW values at the top of the function. So prev is sized to NEW dimensions but gets an ImageData that's the OLD size. 

When imageData.data.length = oldCols * oldRows * 4, but prev.width * prev.height * 4 = newCols * newRows * 4, there are two sub-issues:
1) If old dimensions differ from new dimensions, putImageData may fail or silently truncate/corrupt because the source ImageData doesn't match the destination canvas size.
2) Even if it "works", the subsequent drawImage to temp (which is also sized to NEW dims) draws from an incorrectly-sized source canvas.

The fix should save old dimensions before overwriting:
  const oldCols = cols, oldRows = rows;
  cols = c; rows = r;
  ...
  if (keep && imageData) {
    const prev = document.createElement("canvas");
    prev.width = oldCols;   // use old dims!
    prev.height = oldRows;
    prev.getContext("2d").putImageData(imageData, 0, 0);
    const temp = document.createElement("canvas");
    temp.width = cols;
    temp.height = rows;
    ...

This is a real bug: resize content preservation silently produces incorrect/wrong results when old and new dimensions differ.

=== DEFECT 5 (potential): Right-click eraser via contextmenu only prevents default ===
The code has `canvas.addEventListener("contextmenu", (e) => e.preventDefault())` but never wires up right-button mouse events to the eraser. The hint says "Right-click drag erases" but there is no handler for mousedown/mousemove/mouseup on right-click (button=2). When user right-clicks, contextmenu is prevented (good) but pointerDown won't fire because... actually, it should still fire since contextmenu prevention doesn't cancel mousedown. Let me re-check: The mousedown handler has `if (e.type === "contextmenu") return;` — but that only returns for the contextmenu event itself, not for mouse events. However, pointerDown does NOT check e.button or right-click. So right-click mousedown WILL trigger painting with whatever tool is active (brush, not eraser). The hint says "Right-click drag erases" but there is no mechanism to switch to eraser on right-click.

FIX: Either add `e.button === 2` handling in pointerDown to use eraser temporarily, or update the hint text.

=== SUMMARY ===
Three additional defects to address:
1. Missing touchcancel handler (minor)
2. initCanvas resize content preservation uses wrong canvas dimensions (substantive — content gets garbled when resizing)  
3. Right-click eraser feature described in hint but not implemented

Defect 4 (Ball creation after losing life - one-frame visual glitch):

When the player loses a life (ball falls below canvas), a new ball is created at a fixed position in the "balls.length === 0" block (around line 561):
  balls = [makeBall(CANVAS_SIZE / 2, CANVAS_SIZE - 60)];

This creates the ball at (320, 580) regardless of paddle position. The gameState is then set to "idle", and in the next frame the gameState === "idle" block would set the ball to follow the paddle. But there is a one-frame delay where the ball appears at the fixed position (320, 580) instead of following the paddle.

Expected behavior: The new ball should be created at the paddle's current position (paddle.x + paddle.w / 2, paddle.y - ball.r), matching the idle-state ball-following behavior. This ensures visual consistency - the ball should always appear attached to the paddle before launch, whether it's the initial ball or a ball after losing a life.

The fix should change line 561 from:
  balls = [makeBall(CANVAS_SIZE / 2, CANVAS_SIZE - 60)];
to:
  balls = [makeBall(paddle.x + paddle.w / 2, paddle.y - 7)];

Similarly, the initial ball creation in resetPlay() at line 444 should also follow the paddle:
  balls = [makeBall(paddle.x + paddle.w / 2, paddle.y - 7)];

This would ensure the ball always appears attached to the paddle in idle state, which is the expected Breakout behavior.
